<%# Everything both layouts put in <head>, minus the one thing that differs.

    `csrf_meta_tags` is NOT here. It calls `form_authenticity_token`, which writes
    `session[:_csrf_token]`, which forces a session cookie onto every response that
    renders it. `/` and `/days` are `Cache-Control: public`, and production runs
    behind Thruster's HTTP cache (Dockerfile, 64MB, on by default), so a public
    response carrying a `Set-Cookie` is a shared cache waiting to hand one reader's
    session — and the matching masked CSRF token from the same body — to the next
    one. See specs/0007-no-session-cookie-on-public-pages/.

    The admin layout adds the tag back, because the curator's queue deletes a day
    with `data: { turbo_method: :delete }` and Turbo reads it from the meta tag.
    Reader-facing forms do not need it: `form_with` embeds its own hidden token. %>
<title><%= content_for(:title) || "Tondo — One artwork a day" %></title>
<%# The share button's reveal (story 0031) — first in `<head>`, before
    anything in `<body>` can paint.

    Reads `navigator.userAgent`, not a bridge-set dataset attribute. The
    Hotwire Native Bridge package's own JS only stamps
    `document.documentElement.dataset.bridgeComponents` once its adapter
    finishes a handshake — after module evaluation, not guaranteed before
    first paint. But the STRING it is reading for that handshake,
    `"bridge-components: [...]"`, is already sitting in the User-Agent the
    native shell set on the `WKWebViewConfiguration` before the web view was
    ever created (`hotwire-native-ios`'s `UserAgent.build`, verified against
    the vendored SPM source, not assumed) — the same field
    `ApplicationController#native_shell_version` already parses server-side
    for the auth bridge. `navigator.userAgent` carries that string
    synchronously, before any script runs, so a plain inline script here is
    both simpler and more reliably pre-paint than a bespoke native
    `WKUserScript` would have been (design review D3, corrected by this
    finding at implement time — the plan's original draft proposed the
    native script).

    Split-and-match, not a word-boundary regex: the vendored bridge JS's own
    gate (`BridgeComponent.shouldLoad`) captures the bracketed list and does
    `.split(" ").includes(component)` — an exact space-delimited token
    compare. A word-boundary regex is close but not the same rule (`\bshare\b`
    would also fire inside a future component named e.g. `"photo-share"`,
    which the real gate correctly rejects — code review, adversarial pass).
    Mirroring the actual rule keeps this reveal and the real gate from ever
    disagreeing about which component is registered. %>
<script>
  const match = navigator.userAgent.match(/bridge-components:\s*\[([^\]]*)\]/)
  if (match && match[1].split(" ").includes("share")) {
    document.documentElement.dataset.shellShare = "1"
  }
</script>
<%# `viewport-fit=cover` — story 0008. Without it WebKit insets the layout
    viewport out of the unsafe strips, so `.zoom`'s `position: fixed; inset: 0`
    stops below the notch and above the home indicator and the surround shows
    through as linen bands. DESIGN.md's single accepted exception says that at
    full screen the picture is the room; bands are the wall.

    Every `env(safe-area-inset-*)` in application.css resolved to 0 before this
    line existed — including the one `.zoom__close` was already written against. %>
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1,viewport-fit=cover">
<meta name="description" content="<%= content_for(:description) || "One public-domain artwork a day, drawn from the world's open museum collections, with a short hand-written note on why it matters." %>">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#f4efe6">
<meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
<meta name="application-name" content="Tondo">
<meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
<%= csp_meta_tag %>

<%= yield :head %>

<%# `icon.svg` is listed after the PNG on purpose: a browser that understands
    both takes the vector, which is the only version that stays sharp at the
    16px the tab strip actually draws. The PNG is the fallback and the PWA's
    `purpose: any` icon. `apple-touch-icon` is its own 180 rather than the 512 —
    iOS downloads whatever this points at, and 512 was 4x the bytes for a
    180-point tile. All three come out of `script/brand-render`. %>
<link rel="icon" href="/icon.png" type="image/png">
<link rel="icon" href="/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png">

<%# No font host here, and none is coming back — see specs/0009-self-hosted-fonts/.
    Fraunces and Newsreader are served from this app's own asset pipeline, declared
    in application.css. `test/integration/self_hosted_fonts_test.rb` fails if a
    third-party font host reappears in this file. %>
<%= stylesheet_link_tag :app, "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
<%= javascript_importmap_tags %>
